•   Government and ministries should build their budgets around these numbers;
•   District assemblies should use their own figures, not the national average, to
    decide where the next cedi is spent;
•   Parliament should ask every new programme to state a measurable starting
    point;
•   Employers and investors should read the jobs figures as a market signal, with
    turning informal jobs into formal ones a way to improve job quality worth looking
    at, in a business base where only 7.7 percent of establishments are formal; and
•   Development partners, researchers, civil society, the media and citizens should
    hold institutions to these numbers.

